+++
title: Ancient Egyptian deities
key-facts:
  - name: Worshipped by
    value: Ancient Egyptians
  - name: Culture
    value: Ancient Egypt
  - name: How many gods
    value: More than 1,400 are named in Egyptian texts, by James P. Allen's
      estimate; "thousands upon thousands", by Christian Leitz's
  - name: First written evidence
    value: The Early Dynastic Period, c. 3100–2686 BC
  - name: Part of
    value: Egyptian mythology and the Ancient Egyptian pantheon
  - name: Last formal cults
    value: At Philae, died out in the fifth or sixth century
image-plan:
  - file: La_Tombe_de_Horemheb_cropped.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "Osiris, Anubis and Horus painted in the Tomb of Horemheb (KV57) in
      the Valley of the Kings: a god with green skin, a jackal-headed god and
      a falcon-headed one."
  - file: Narmer-Tjet2.JPG
    role: inline
    section: origins
    caption: "Narmer, a Predynastic ruler, accompanied by men carrying the
      standards of various local gods. In the Predynastic and Early Dynastic
      Periods, gods were often represented by such standards: poles topped by
      emblems of deities."
  - file: Early_dynastic_statue_of_a_squatting_baboon_02.jpg
    role: inline
    section: origins
    caption: A statue of the baboon god Hedj-Wer, inscribed with the name of king
      Narmer.
  - file: Statuetta_di_Tauret_dedicata_dal_disegnatore_Parahotep_SA63826.tif
    role: inline
    section: roles
    caption: A statuette of Taweret, made between 1292 and 1190 BC in the New
      Kingdom. She began with a minor, demon-like role and came over time to
      be credited with great influence. Museo Egizio, Turin.
  - file: Goddess_Nut_2.JPG
    role: full
    section: behavior
    caption: The sky goddess Nut swallows the sun, which travels through her body
      at night to be reborn at dawn.
  - file: Edfu_Temple_032010_21_d2.jpg
    role: inline
    section: locations
    caption: Deities personifying provinces of Egypt. Each nome had its own set of
      gods, as did each hour of the day and night.
  - file: BD_Sokar-Osiris.jpg
    role: inline
    section: names-and-epithets
    caption: Seker-Osiris standing in a shrine, in a facsimile of a vignette from
      the Papyrus of Ani. Egyptian texts connected the funerary god Seker with
      Osiris, the most important funerary deity.
  - file: Nun_and_Naunet.jpg
    role: inline
    section: gender-and-sexuality
    caption: Naunet and Nu, from Deir el Medina. Deities often form male and
      female pairs.
  - file: Ramesses-Ptah-Sekhmet.jpg
    role: inline
    section: relationships
    caption: "Ptah and Sekhmet flank the king, who takes the role of their child,
      Nefertum: the family triad of Memphis, with the pharaoh standing in the
      son's place."
  - file: Amun-Ra_kamutef_2.jpg
    role: inline
    section: manifestations-and-combinations
    caption: Amun-Ra-Kamutef, a form of Amun with the solar characteristics of Ra
      and the procreative powers connected with Min. The solar disk on his
      headdress is taken from Ra, and his erect phallus comes from the
      iconography of Min.
  - file: Bes-E_11554-IMG_0579-white_(cropped).jpg
    role: inline
    section: unity-of-the-divine-in-traditional-religion
    caption: Bes wearing the attributes of many other deities. Images like this
      one represent the presence of a multitude of divine powers within a
      single being.
  - file: Hathor-E_26023-IMG_4152-black_cropped.jpg
    role: full
    section: descriptions-and-depictions
    caption: "Four forms of Hathor on a single Late Period (664 – 332 BC) statue:
      a cow with a sun disk between her horns (above center); a human with a
      headdress shaped like a sistrum (left); a human body with a lioness's
      head (right); and a rearing serpent with a woman's head (below center)."
  - file: Le_roi_Ramsès_parmi_les_dieux_(Louvre,_B_13).jpg
    role: inline
    section: relationship-with-the-pharaoh
    caption: Horus offers life to the pharaoh, Ramesses II. Painted limestone,
      circa 1275 BC, 19th dynasty, from the small temple built by Ramses II in
      Abydos. Louvre museum, Paris, France.
  - file: Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III12.JPG
    role: inline
    section: relationship-with-the-pharaoh
    caption: Ramesses III presents offerings to Amun. According to royal ideology,
      the rituals priests usually performed were the king's own work.
  - file: Flickr_-_archer10_(Dennis)_-_Egypt-10C-024.jpg
    role: full
    section: presence-in-the-human-world
    caption: Ramesses II (second from right) seated with the gods Ptah, Amun, and
      Ra in the sanctuary of the Great Temple at Abu Simbel.
  - file: RPM_Ägypten_240.jpg
    role: inline
    section: intervention-in-human-lives
    caption: An amulet of the god Shed, who emerged in the New Kingdom to
      represent divine rescue from harm.
  - file: Egyptian_-_Horus-Stele_-_Walters_22332.jpg
    role: inline
    section: worship
    caption: A stela of Horus on the Crocodiles, showing the deity triumphing over
      danger. From the New Kingdom to Roman times, Egyptians drank water that
      had been poured over such stelae, to ingest Horus's curative power.
  - file: Stèle_de_la_dame_Tapéret_-_Musée_du_Louvre_Antiquités_égyptiennes_E_52_;_N_3663_-_photo_3.jpg
    role: inline
    section: worship
    caption: A woman worships Ra-Horakhty, who blesses her with rays of light.
  - file: Museo_Barracco_-_Giove_Ammone_1010637.JPG
    role: inline
    section: worship
    caption: Jupiter Ammon, a combination of Amun and the Roman god Jupiter.
further:
  - slug: ancient-egyptian-religion
    why: The beliefs and rituals surrounding these gods formed its core, and it
      emerged sometime in prehistory.
  - slug: egyptian-mythology
    why: "Where the gods act: a vaguely imagined past in which they were present
      on earth and dealt with humans face to face."
  - slug: ancient-egyptian-creation-myths
    why: Several accounts of the first divine act, each focused on a different
      creator god, and the Egyptians saw no contradiction between them.
